From: Andrew Berry <andrew@furrypaws.ca>
Subject: fping parser: reject a sample whose fields are not numbers

Upstream's fping arm treats ANY line of 12 whitespace fields arriving on the
pinger fifo as a reflector response, then feeds field 6 to `printf %.3f` and
field 0 to `10#${...}`. Neither field is checked. A line that satisfies the
count but not the shape therefore reaches bash arithmetic, and under the
`set -u` the daemon runs with, the resulting unbound-variable abort exits the
process -- cleanly, so no procd respawn policy catches it, and the WAN silently
runs unshaped until someone restarts the service by hand.

Reported against v3.2.2 with pinger_binary=fping, three errors one iteration
apart and then the daemon gone:

    line 1292: printf: bytes: invalid number
    line 1354: ((: (t_start_us - 10#S1786559486685130: value too great for base
    line 1525: S1786559486685130: unbound variable

Field 6 held the literal word `bytes` and field 0 held `S` glued to a
timestamp, i.e. the reader had picked up a spliced line: the daemon's own
`SARS <dl> <ul>` load message shares ${main_fd} with the pinger, so a torn
write leaves a frankenline whose field count still passes the gate.

Upstream fixed the sibling case in PR #392 (a backwards clock step makes fping
print a negative rtt, which `10#` cannot represent) by validating field 6.
That is merged to master but is NOT in any release -- v3.2.2 is still the
newest tag -- so it is carried here, extended to field 0, which the report
above proves is just as reachable.

Drop this patch on the upstream bump that first ships PR #392, and re-check
whether the timestamp half went upstream too.

Upstream PR: https://github.com/lynxthecat/cake-autorate/pull/392

--- a/cake-autorate.sh
+++ b/cake-autorate.sh
@@ -1206,7 +1206,31 @@
 					fi
 					;;
 				fping)
-					if ((${#command[@]} == 12))
+					# Upstream trusts every 12-field line the pinger fifo yields.
+					# That is not safe. fping reports a NEGATIVE rtt when the clock
+					# steps backwards mid-ping (upstream PR #392), and a line spliced
+					# in the fifo -- the daemon's own "SARS" load message shares
+					# ${main_fd} with the pinger -- can leave an arbitrary word in any
+					# field while the field COUNT still passes this gate. Either one
+					# reaches printf %.3f and 10#${...} below, and under `set -u` the
+					# resulting unbound-variable abort takes the whole daemon down:
+					# the WAN then runs unshaped, with a clean exit no procd respawn
+					# policy catches.
+					#
+					# So gate the two fields the arithmetic actually consumes. Glob
+					# class checks, not [[ =~ ]]: an ERE recompiles on every sample,
+					# and this runs once per ping per reflector. command[6] is the rtt
+					# (digits and dots only, which also rejects a sign or exponent);
+					# command[0] is the bracketed timestamp, checked in the same
+					# stripped form the parse below feeds to 10#, and required to be
+					# non-empty so an all-punctuation token cannot become "10#".
+					#
+					# A rejected line is simply not a reflector response, so no new
+					# control flow: the sample is dropped and the owd baselines never
+					# see it.
+					if ((${#command[@]} == 12)) &&
+						[[ ${command[6]} != *[!0-9.]* ]] &&
+						[[ -n ${command[0]//[\[\].]} && ${command[0]//[\[\].]} != *[!0-9]* ]]
 					then
 						timestamp=${command[0]} reflector=${command[1]} seq=${command[3]} rtt_ms=${command[6]} reflector_response=1
 					fi
